Algorithm
Part A: Hamming Code Encoding
1. Start.
2. Read the 8-bit input data from the user.
3. Allocate 12 bits for the Hamming code.
4. Place the 8 data bits in positions 3, 5, 6, 7, 9, 10, 11 and 12.
5. Reserve positions 1, 2, 4 and 8 for parity bits.
6. Calculate the first parity bit using positions 1, 3, 5, 7, 9 and 11.
7. Calculate the second parity bit using positions 2, 3, 6, 7, 10 and 11.
8. Calculate the third parity bit using positions 4, 5, 6, 7 and 12.
9. Calculate the fourth parity bit using positions 8, 9, 10, 11 and 12.
10. Generate the 12-bit Hamming code.
11. Store the encoded codeword in memory.
Part B: Error Detection and Correction
12. Read the encoded 12-bit codeword from memory.
13. Introduce an error at a selected bit position (optional, for testing).
14. Calculate the parity checks again.
15. Calculate the syndrome using the four parity-check results.
16. Check whether the syndrome is zero.
17. If the syndrome is zero, no error is detected.
18. If the syndrome is non-zero, convert the syndrome into a decimal error position.
19. Identify the incorrect bit position.
20. Flip the incorrect bit to correct the error.
21. Store the corrected codeword in another memory location.
22. Extract the original 8-bit data from the corrected codeword.
23. Store the recovered data in memory.
24. Display the original data, encoded codeword, syndrome and corrected data.
25. Stop.


